# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VOLTOP WEB · variables de entorno
#
# The two NEXT_PUBLIC_ ones are optional: both default to the production value,
# so the project starts and builds without this file. They exist so a deployment
# that is NOT production does not behave as if it were.
#
# Usage: copy to `.env.local` (which is gitignored) and adjust.
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# THERE ARE SECRETS IN THIS PROJECT NOW, and this header used to say the
# opposite — it claimed the B2B form went out by `mailto:` and that nothing
# needed a key. Since 2026-09-09 the form posts to `/api/leads`, which sends
# through Amazon SES with IAM credentials. Those credentials go in `.env.local`
# and NEVER in this file or in the repository.
#
# In CI they no longer come from Doppler: the three AWS variables there were
# deleted. The SES key pair comes from GITHUB secrets, named per environment
# (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID_SES_PRD` / `..._STG`) because GitHub has a single flat
# namespace; the region is a literal in the workflow, since it is not a secret.
# Doppler still holds `GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY` and `GCP_SA_KEY_LANDING`.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Dominio con el que se construyen sitemap, canonical y hreflang.
# Defecto: https://voltop.co
#
# IMPORTANTE en staging y en previsualizaciones: sin esta variable, esas
# páginas le dicen a un buscador que su contenido canónico vive en producción.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://voltop.co

# Contenedor de Google Tag Manager.
# Defecto: GTM-WJ5S2LBF (producción)
#
# En staging conviene apuntarlo a un contenedor de pruebas o dejarlo vacío:
# el tráfico de pruebas en la analítica real no se puede deshacer.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GTM_ID=GTM-WJ5S2LBF

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Amazon SES · the B2B lead form
#
# The sender and the recipients are NOT here — they live in
# `core/business/infrastructure/email/leadDelivery.ts`, because they change for
# reasons that are not technical and putting them in a secret store would mean a
# rebuild and a redeploy to alter an address. The region is in both places: the
# environment wins, and that file holds the fallback so an unset variable
# degrades to the right region instead of to an empty string.
#
# THE `_SES` SUFFIX MATTERS. The pair belongs to an IAM identity that can send
# mail and nothing else; the standard `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` name would put it in
# the SDK's ambient credential chain, where any other AWS client in the process
# would pick it up. Named this way, only the mailer reads it.
#
# In CI they come from GITHUB secrets, with the environment appended to the name
# (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID_SES_PRD`) because GitHub has one flat namespace. Only the
# `_PRD` pair exists, so STAGING DOES NOT SEND — deliberately: the alternative,
# reusing the production pair, mails the commercial team on every test.
#
# Doppler still holds `GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY` and `GCP_SA_KEY_LANDING`; the
# three AWS variables it used to hold were deleted.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Optional in the sense that the SDK falls back to an IAM role or a shared
# profile when they are absent — which is what lets this run under a workload
# identity one day. Absent AND with no role, the form cannot send.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID_SES=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_SES=

# Optional too. In CI it comes from the GitHub secret `AWS_REGION_PRD` — no
# `_SES` in that one: it is the region of the account, not of the key pair.
# Unset, the code uses the region recorded in `leadDelivery.ts`.
AWS_REGION=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Google Sheets · the record of every lead
#
# OPTIONAL. Without it the form still sends its mail and simply writes no row,
# which is what a laptop or a preview should do. WHICH spreadsheet is not here
# either — it is in `core/business/infrastructure/sheets/leadSheetTarget.ts`,
# because a spreadsheet id is not a credential: holding it opens nothing unless
# the document is shared with you.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The service account's JSON key, PREFERABLY BASE64-ENCODED:
#
#   base64 -i clave-de-la-cuenta-de-servicio.json | tr -d '\n'
#
# Raw JSON also works and is fine locally. Base64 is what should go in Doppler:
# the key is multi-line, it travels to the container as a Docker build argument,
# and a build argument that loses its newlines produces a private key truncated
# at the first one — which fails much later, as `invalid_grant`, nowhere near
# the cause.
#
# The service account also needs two things done OUTSIDE this file: the Google
# Sheets API enabled on its project, and the spreadsheet SHARED WITH ITS EMAIL
# as an editor. Without the share, Google answers 403 and it reads like a bug
# in the code.
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY=
